1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,440
They aren't human, but they use tools, build intricate structures, devise diabolical traps

2
00:00:08,441 --> 00:00:12,560
to capture prey, and are expert farmers.

3
00:00:12,561 --> 00:00:16,840
Terms like bird brain and monkey business take on a whole new meaning.

4
00:00:16,841 --> 00:00:20,080
Now, more Nature's Engineers on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,200
For centuries, we've often reflected that our superior intelligence has given rise to

6
00:00:37,201 --> 00:00:43,000
an exclusive ability to perform tasks by creating and using tools.

7
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:48,440
What's brought us to the pinnacle and put us on the top of the food chain is our ability

8
00:00:48,441 --> 00:00:52,260
to create things with which we modify the environment.

9
00:00:52,261 --> 00:00:55,180
So if you need to make a hole, you know, we invented the drill.

10
00:00:55,181 --> 00:00:59,360
If you needed to get a nail into a piece of wood, first it was a rock and then it turned

11
00:00:59,361 --> 00:01:00,360
into a hammer.

12
00:01:00,361 --> 00:01:07,700
But it turns out that we're not the only tool users with the advantage of hands, with

13
00:01:07,701 --> 00:01:12,640
the dexterity made possible by an opposable thumb, and big brains.

14
00:01:12,641 --> 00:01:18,800
These are traits we share with the great apes.

15
00:01:18,801 --> 00:01:23,180
For centuries, the great apes were lumped in with monkeys and thought of as primitive

16
00:01:23,181 --> 00:01:25,460
and often frightening.

17
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:30,820
They could also be amusing if they were tamed and trained to mimic people.

18
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:40,140
Not so long ago, human perception of chimpanzees were these were just silly animals for our entertainment.

19
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:43,120
And you would see that in television shows and movies.

20
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:46,400
They'd be dressed up in clothes, doing tricks even at zoos.

21
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:53,620
Like a parrot trained to talk, any skills they might happen to demonstrate

22
00:01:53,621 --> 00:01:55,820
would be attributed to their human role models.

23
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:58,300
Monkey C, Monkey D.

24
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:02,980
Fortunately, nowadays, our perception of them has changed.

25
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:08,520
In 1960, Jane Goodall began the longest-running research project of wild chimpanzees.

26
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:13,000
She discovered that they actually fashion, modify, and use tools.

27
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:18,620
This actually made us, humans, redefine our uniqueness.

28
00:02:18,621 --> 00:02:23,900
And so this changed the perception of chimpanzees and other great apes.

29
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:30,440
Modern science has shown chimpanzee DNA to be nearly 99% the same as humans.

30
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,180
They are the most gregarious of the great apes,

31
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:37,220
making them the easiest to study closely in the wild.

32
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:44,820
They use thin sticks as probes to poke into termite mounds.

33
00:02:44,821 --> 00:02:49,920
The termites bite the invading stick and are pulled out to become a protein-rich snack.

34
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:59,620
To crack open hard-shelled nuts, chimpanzees use two tools at once, a hammer and an anvil.

35
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:05,800
That actually not only embodies a quite interesting principle,

36
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,420
it needs to be conducted in a skillful kind of a way.

37
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:13,540
It's a bit like remembering eating nuts at Christmas and using the nutcrackers.

38
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:18,360
If you apply the nutcrackers too vigorously, then you get a completely smashed nut.

39
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:21,000
So you smash up not only the shell, but you smash up the kernel.

40
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:31,560
So there's a kind of way of hitting the nut when it's sitting on the anvil in such a way that it just cracks it over nicely and you get a nice clean kernel out of it.

41
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:40,340
Chimpanzees have also been observed creating and using a sponge-like object to soak up water from hard-to-reach places.

42
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,800
What they'll do in the wild is they'll take leaves and crunch them up into balls,

43
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:50,040
similar to this fibrous browse ball here.

44
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:53,720
This has been chewed by the chimps and sucked of all its nutrients.

45
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:57,580
So they can dip this in, use it as a sponge, and then soak out the water.

46
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,300
They don't necessarily use this behavior in captivity because they have water sources,

47
00:04:02,301 --> 00:04:06,680
but this sponge-using behavior has been observed in wild chimpanzees.

48
00:04:06,681 --> 00:04:13,680
Orangutans have also been observed using sticks to probe for termites and other treats.

49
00:04:13,681 --> 00:04:19,740
If the stick they find is too big, they will modify their tool by stripping off or chewing

50
00:04:19,741 --> 00:04:21,960
away part of it to make the end smaller.

51
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:27,180
It's because of the structure of their hands that they're able to manipulate objects,

52
00:04:27,181 --> 00:04:30,560
to use sticks and branches, and to dig into objects.

53
00:04:30,561 --> 00:04:35,500
You'll also notice that they usually begin using their tongue or their long fingers,

54
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:40,920
And then they go to the sticks to try to get the object out.

55
00:04:40,921 --> 00:04:46,880
Because they are so oriented to using tools, as keepers we have to actually really be careful

56
00:04:46,881 --> 00:04:52,260
that we don't give them branches that are too long, that they will create a lot of mischief.

57
00:04:52,261 --> 00:04:56,000
They've taken apart our filter system at our pool using objects.

58
00:04:56,001 --> 00:05:02,160
They're very curious, very mischievous, and it's amazing how dexterous they are once they

59
00:05:02,161 --> 00:05:05,680
They have like maybe a stick, a piece of browse that they've whittled down, and they'll actually

60
00:05:05,681 --> 00:05:07,120
make a nice point to it.

61
00:05:07,240 --> 00:05:08,440
So we have to be very careful.

62
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:15,060
In captivity, tool use is now encouraged for mental stimulation and to fight boredom.

63
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:21,240
At the Los Angeles Zoo, the ape's tool-using efforts are rewarded with tasty treats like

64
00:05:21,241 --> 00:05:22,260
peanut butter and yogurt.

65
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:27,700
But in their natural habitats, these abilities can be an important life skill to acquire.

66
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:30,640
Tool using is a necessity to survival.

67
00:05:30,641 --> 00:05:34,360
There is a problem that needs to be solved, and so let's use a tool to do it.

68
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:37,820
We see it in our behavior, and we see it in chimpanzee behavior,

69
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:43,320
such as using rocks to crack open nuts or the twigs to fish for termites and ants.

70
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:47,380
These are nutrients that they need in their diet, and it is a necessity.

71
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:53,280
When looking into the eyes of one of the great apes, you can't help but wonder, what are they thinking?

72
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:58,380
You know, ape intelligence has often been compared to human intelligence.

73
00:05:58,381 --> 00:06:01,560
They try to put an age upon it.

74
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:04,380
Are they as smart as a five-year-old or a seven-year-old?

75
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:05,840
I hear this all the time out here.

76
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:08,720
Personally, I don't like to do that.

77
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:12,420
I like to think of them as intelligent as they need to be for their environment.

78
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:19,500
And we need to view them as unique individual beings with an extreme level of intelligence.

79
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:26,300
Who's to say that they've reached the pinnacle of their development as some of nature's most skilled engineers?

80
00:06:26,301 --> 00:06:31,360
or what skills they will acquire thousands of years into the future.

81
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:37,000
One day, this really might become a planet of the apes.

82
00:06:38,840 --> 00:06:42,940
While having almost human hands gives the great apes a distinct advantage,

83
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:49,300
it's amazing what other animals can accomplish with just jaws and claws.

84
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:54,600
The stones used by some chimpanzees to crack nuts

85
00:06:54,601 --> 00:06:57,940
are similar to the chopping tools used by our human ancestors

86
00:06:57,941 --> 00:07:00,860
more than 2.5 million years ago.

87
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:05,980
From subways

88
00:07:05,981 --> 00:07:08,020
to tunnels

89
00:07:08,021 --> 00:07:10,360
and mines,

90
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:13,580
we have carved out a partial underground existence

91
00:07:13,581 --> 00:07:17,780
and have created everything from basic tools like the pick and shovel

92
00:07:17,781 --> 00:07:20,400
to huge boring machines,

93
00:07:21,060 --> 00:07:23,120
enabling us to dig deep beneath the earth.

94
00:07:23,121 --> 00:07:28,120
But we aren't the only mammals that create subterranean structures.

95
00:07:28,121 --> 00:07:31,120
A variety of species use their claws and jaws

96
00:07:31,121 --> 00:07:34,120
to excavate everything from simple burrows

97
00:07:34,121 --> 00:07:36,120
to intricate tunnel systems.

98
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:43,120
There are tunneling mammals on every continent except Antarctica.

99
00:07:43,121 --> 00:07:47,120
Africa has some of the most exotic species, including meerkats.

100
00:07:47,121 --> 00:07:50,120
Meerkats are only found in South Africa,

101
00:07:50,121 --> 00:07:57,300
and they're a desert species that will prey on anything that they can possibly overpower.

102
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:01,020
And they'll take things like scorpions and venomous snakes and kill them and eat them.

103
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:07,000
Even though they are skilled hunters, they need to build burrows to take refuge from larger predators

104
00:08:07,001 --> 00:08:09,760
and to provide a safe place to raise their young.

105
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:14,520
Each meerkat community is called a mob and can number up to 40.

106
00:08:15,420 --> 00:08:18,560
Using their powerful forelegs and long, sharp claws to dig,

107
00:08:18,561 --> 00:08:24,460
Each mob will excavate underground dwellings consisting of entrance holes, tunnels, and sleeping chambers.

108
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:33,240
Their sleeping chambers are usually six to eight feet underground, deep enough to keep them cooler in the summer and warmer in the winter.

109
00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:40,460
There may be up to 70 different entrances to each burrow system, offering plenty of ventilation and escape options.

110
00:08:40,461 --> 00:08:47,600
They have to dig new homes often to avoid the inevitable buildup of fleas and other

111
00:08:47,601 --> 00:08:51,700
parasites that accumulate in their dens.

112
00:08:51,701 --> 00:08:55,620
While meerkats spend most of their time each day above ground hunting and soaking up the

113
00:08:55,621 --> 00:09:01,500
sun of the African desert, naked mole rats have evolved and adapted to the point they

114
00:09:01,501 --> 00:09:07,180
never need to leave the safety of their underground existence.

115
00:09:07,181 --> 00:09:11,660
They don't need claws to dig because they've developed outsized pairs of constantly growing

116
00:09:11,661 --> 00:09:17,080
teeth that enable them to gnaw their way through hard soil.

117
00:09:17,081 --> 00:09:21,620
Their narrowest tunnels can be less than two inches wide, but one of the advantages of

118
00:09:21,621 --> 00:09:26,760
being furless is that they can easily squeeze past each other.

119
00:09:26,761 --> 00:09:32,700
Nearly blind, they're an example of how far specialization can go.

120
00:09:32,701 --> 00:09:37,660
Because of the lack of extremes in climate in the part of Central Africa where they dwell,

121
00:09:37,661 --> 00:09:43,380
they've deviated from one of the main definitions of what it is to be a mammal.

122
00:09:43,381 --> 00:09:47,940
Naked mole rats are actually cold-blooded in the sense that if you take them out of

123
00:09:47,941 --> 00:09:50,980
their burrow and put them in a warm room, they just heat up.

124
00:09:50,981 --> 00:09:53,780
If you put them in a cold room, they cool down.

125
00:09:53,781 --> 00:10:00,740
They have no physiological mechanism left for regulating their body temperature.

126
00:10:00,741 --> 00:10:05,447
social structure is similar to communal insects, like bees and termites.

127
00:10:05,448 --> 00:10:06,820
There's one queen per

128
00:10:06,821 --> 00:10:12,489
group and a division of labor among the rest, including soldiers and workers.

129
00:10:12,490 --> 00:10:13,520
Raymond Mendez

130
00:10:13,521 --> 00:10:17,600
became fascinated with these unique animals and brought some home with him to the American

131
00:10:17,601 --> 00:10:18,301
Southwest.

132
00:10:18,302 --> 00:10:23,460
They've become an important part of his business of creating exhibits and breeding

133
00:10:23,461 --> 00:10:25,301
specimens for museums and zoos.

134
00:10:25,302 --> 00:10:29,340
Here we are at the studio, and look at this little baby. This is one

135
00:10:29,341 --> 00:10:34,920
of the mole rats that I've raised from the ones that first came in from Africa many, many years

136
00:10:34,921 --> 00:10:39,401
ago. And you can see everything that you might want to right there.

137
00:10:39,402 --> 00:10:41,140
Look, there's their teeth.

138
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:42,989
See how big they are.

139
00:10:42,990 --> 00:10:47,460
And also look at the mouth parts, how they close up so the dirt doesn't go

140
00:10:47,461 --> 00:10:49,893
into their mouth but falls on the sides.

141
00:10:49,894 --> 00:10:53,360
And you'll notice they do have hairs, little bitty hairs,

142
00:10:53,361 --> 00:10:56,160
but they're more hairs for sensing the edge of the burrow,

143
00:10:56,520 --> 00:11:00,300
not for keeping themselves warm or cold or anything else.

144
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:04,280
The eyes are very, very small, just barely little dots,

145
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:06,540
and the ears are reduced.

146
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:08,080
Look at them there. They're just these little openings,

147
00:11:08,180 --> 00:11:11,060
but they hear very well because that's how they communicate in the nest.

148
00:11:11,740 --> 00:11:13,960
In the darkness of their underground world,

149
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:17,840
they make chirping sounds to locate each other and coordinate their activities.

150
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:19,940
Well, actually, they sing.

151
00:11:19,941 --> 00:11:28,380
If you keep mole rats or if you lived with mole rats and actually put a probe underneath them and listen, you'd hear all these little bird calls going out.

152
00:11:28,780 --> 00:11:36,460
And these calls travel long distances down the tunnels and they actually tell you where there's food, where we are, come this way.

153
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:43,640
If there's somebody who attacks, there's a very specific kind of a grunt and call that will bring in soldiers.

154
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:46,440
So they're always communicating underground.

155
00:11:46,441 --> 00:11:50,680
When excavating new tunnels, naked mole rats work as a team.

156
00:11:50,681 --> 00:11:54,680
The one in front chomps into the soil like a mini steam shovel.

157
00:11:54,681 --> 00:12:01,160
The naked mole rats have enormous jaws where over 40% of the musculature of their body

158
00:12:01,161 --> 00:12:02,160
is focused.

159
00:12:02,161 --> 00:12:06,880
So they grind away at the soil and then they kick it back underneath them.

160
00:12:06,881 --> 00:12:11,300
So they make a long conveyor belt of bodies, each one kicking back and the one that they

161
00:12:11,301 --> 00:12:15,180
kick towards raises up so the other one can go underneath them.

162
00:12:15,181 --> 00:12:19,280
So they're making this conveyor belt that's constantly moving back, and they move it through

163
00:12:19,281 --> 00:12:21,180
the soil for hundreds of feet.

164
00:12:21,181 --> 00:12:25,520
And when it finally gets to an area where they're kicking it up into a volcano, what

165
00:12:25,521 --> 00:12:27,740
they do is there's a kicker.

166
00:12:27,741 --> 00:12:32,720
And the kicker just sits there with his legs straight up, facing down, and everybody throws

167
00:12:32,721 --> 00:12:36,460
dirt under the kicker, and the kicker just kicks up, and the stuff just comes pluming

168
00:12:36,461 --> 00:12:41,700
out of the dirt like a little mini volcano.

169
00:12:41,701 --> 00:12:46,720
The main reason they keep digging is they're constantly searching for enough roots and tubers to feed the colony.

170
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:51,080
Naked mole rat colonies can have over 100 individuals in them,

171
00:12:51,260 --> 00:12:55,700
and their tunnel systems expand over areas as big as a football field.

172
00:12:56,620 --> 00:13:02,660
And as a matter of fact, that's one of the ways that they think new mole rat colonies are formed.

173
00:13:02,661 --> 00:13:08,840
In other words, one of these branches that's out looking for food might have a collapse in the tunnel system,

174
00:13:08,841 --> 00:13:12,040
and they might not be able to find their way back to the main colony.

175
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:15,940
While the tunneling prowess of naked mole rats is impressive,

176
00:13:16,460 --> 00:13:19,180
when it comes to digging complex and vast systems,

177
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:24,000
prairie dogs, rodents native to North America, are unsurpassed.

178
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:27,140
They don't work as a team like naked mole rats,

179
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:29,760
but the individuals work for the good of the group.

180
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:33,640
Each group or family of prairie dogs is called a coterie.

181
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:38,620
When several families live close together, it's referred to as a prairie dog town.

182
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:45,440
At the turn of the last century, before we encroached on their territory, there was a

183
00:13:45,441 --> 00:13:53,080
gigantic prairie dog town in Texas that would make New York City seem puny.

184
00:13:53,081 --> 00:13:59,000
This sprawling, rodent-filled metropolis was estimated to cover 25,000 square miles,

185
00:13:59,001 --> 00:14:03,720
and contained up to 400 million prairie dogs.

186
00:14:03,721 --> 00:14:06,720
Right behind me is a group of black-tailed prairie dogs.

187
00:14:06,721 --> 00:14:10,760
These were purchased for the zoo a number of years ago.

188
00:14:10,761 --> 00:14:14,720
Prairie dogs are considered an agricultural pest in the state of California.

189
00:14:14,721 --> 00:14:16,440
They're not really found here.

190
00:14:16,441 --> 00:14:24,700
They're found in Utah, Colorado, Wyoming, Texas, New Mexico, and in that area in the

191
00:14:24,701 --> 00:14:26,280
Great Plains state.

192
00:14:26,281 --> 00:14:32,760
Prairie dog burrows may be anywhere from 3 to 14 feet deep, and as long as 100 yards

193
00:14:32,761 --> 00:14:36,020
of actual tunneling space.

194
00:14:36,021 --> 00:14:43,560
So a prairie dog may be going from these very depths of 8 to 12 feet and backing all of

195
00:14:43,561 --> 00:14:48,740
this soil out to the edge of the coterie.

196
00:14:48,741 --> 00:14:53,460
Their tunnels lead to several hollowed out areas, each with its own particular purpose.

197
00:14:53,461 --> 00:14:59,060
Once we're into the prairie dog burrow, one of the first chambers you may find is a listening

198
00:14:59,061 --> 00:15:00,340
chamber.

199
00:15:00,341 --> 00:15:06,680
And this is where the prairie dogs can actually hear any of the predators that might be out

200
00:15:06,681 --> 00:15:08,000
above ground.

201
00:15:08,001 --> 00:15:13,080
For instance, something like a badger that is trying to get in and enlarge the hole.

202
00:15:13,081 --> 00:15:17,680
From there, there may be another chamber or two that are sort of turnaround tunnels, that

203
00:15:17,681 --> 00:15:19,580
are dead end tunnels.

204
00:15:19,581 --> 00:15:22,180
And there's one room that they use for a toilet.

205
00:15:22,181 --> 00:15:27,480
There's one room that they use as a nursery where the babies are born and it's filled

206
00:15:27,481 --> 00:15:29,460
with grass cuttings and what have you.

207
00:15:29,461 --> 00:15:31,893
nice soft warm spot for mom and the kids.

208
00:15:31,894 --> 00:15:34,860
I'm in the middle of a prairie dog ward or coterie

209
00:15:34,861 --> 00:15:41,294
and in front of me is a fine example of a prairie dog entrance burrow.

210
00:15:41,295 --> 00:15:43,960
See how mounded the front is?

211
00:15:44,520 --> 00:15:52,360
Prairie dogs do this by pounding their nose to compact the soil around the top of the entrance

212
00:15:52,361 --> 00:15:57,182
burrow. The mounding on the outside of an entrance burrow is threefold.

213
00:15:57,183 --> 00:15:59,220
One, it serves as a heightened

214
00:15:59,221 --> 00:16:04,303
area for a sentry to stand at the top and look for predators.

215
00:16:04,304 --> 00:16:06,220
Secondly, it also helps

216
00:16:06,221 --> 00:16:11,038
if in case of torrential rain, the burrow will not get flooded.

217
00:16:11,039 --> 00:16:12,720
And finally, it serves

218
00:16:12,721 --> 00:16:14,303
as ventilation for the coterie.

219
00:16:14,304 --> 00:16:17,060
If you place your hand right over the top of the hole,

220
00:16:17,061 --> 00:16:22,440
you can actually feel the air movement coming out of the hole.

221
00:16:22,441 --> 00:16:26,248
Most of their tunnels have two entrances, one built higher than the other.

222
00:16:26,249 --> 00:16:27,020
An above-ground

223
00:16:27,021 --> 00:16:30,020
the breeze blows across the higher hole faster than the lower hole,

224
00:16:30,021 --> 00:16:32,020
causing a partial vacuum.

225
00:16:32,021 --> 00:16:37,020
This causes air to enter the lower hole and blow out of the higher domed crater.

226
00:16:37,021 --> 00:16:40,020
But does this mean that prairie dogs are experts at air conditioning as well?

227
00:16:40,021 --> 00:16:43,020
The prairie dogs, in building their burrow systems,

228
00:16:43,021 --> 00:16:47,020
don't need to understand the principle upon which it works.

229
00:16:47,021 --> 00:16:55,020
We can imagine it evolving simply by saying that in the distant past,

230
00:16:55,021 --> 00:17:01,980
Prairie dogs or their ancestors that built burrow systems which ventilated well,

231
00:17:02,540 --> 00:17:10,440
were more successful, produced more offspring than individuals that produced poor ventilation systems.

232
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:14,560
Above ground, prairie dogs frequently chatter to each other.

233
00:17:15,300 --> 00:17:18,540
Some scientists now believe the various sounds are specific words,

234
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:20,460
conveying their conditions and surroundings.

235
00:17:21,400 --> 00:17:24,380
While the complexity of their language is still open to debate,

236
00:17:24,381 --> 00:17:29,380
There's no uncertainty about the meaning when the watchdog stationed at the top of the mound speaks.

237
00:17:29,381 --> 00:17:34,380
If a predator approaches, he will bark out a warning so the rest can run for cover.

238
00:17:34,381 --> 00:17:36,380
Hence the name prairie dog.

239
00:17:36,381 --> 00:17:43,380
They'll stand up on their hind legs and make this yip, yip noise.

240
00:17:47,380 --> 00:17:51,380
Then boom, that is everybody takes safe refuge in the burrows.

241
00:17:51,381 --> 00:17:53,380
And so that's what they end up doing.

242
00:17:53,381 --> 00:17:57,560
And then, after 10 or 15 minutes, depending on when they feel it's safe,

243
00:17:57,980 --> 00:18:00,740
little heads will start popping up and they'll see the coast is clear.

244
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:05,080
Okay, let's go back to our feeding and grooming and lazing out in the sun.

245
00:18:05,960 --> 00:18:08,960
Ever since the settlement of North America's Great Plains,

246
00:18:09,620 --> 00:18:12,240
ranchers and farmers have considered prairie dogs pests

247
00:18:12,241 --> 00:18:14,180
that ate the same grasses as cattle.

248
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:16,320
They have been hunted and poisoned,

249
00:18:16,940 --> 00:18:19,260
and over time their numbers have been greatly reduced.

250
00:18:20,220 --> 00:18:21,400
Luckily for prairie dogs,

251
00:18:21,401 --> 00:18:25,160
many now consider their position in the ecosystem of the plains less harmful,

252
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:28,580
and they're not being eradicated as quickly as they were in the past.

253
00:18:30,120 --> 00:18:33,340
While some animals go underground to avoid being hunted,

254
00:18:34,160 --> 00:18:38,700
others do just the opposite, burrowing to become expert hunters.

255
00:18:40,460 --> 00:18:43,080
The name given to naked mole rats is misleading.

256
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:46,840
They are in fact more closely related to porcupines and guinea pigs

257
00:18:46,841 --> 00:18:48,920
than to either moles or rats.

258
00:18:51,400 --> 00:19:04,400
We've become successful hunters in large part because of the hunting tools we've engineered.

259
00:19:04,401 --> 00:19:11,400
But like us, there are some very special members of the animal kingdom that also engineer devices

260
00:19:11,401 --> 00:19:19,400
to augment their hunting capabilities.

261
00:19:19,401 --> 00:19:24,400
Spider webs are probably the most commonly thought of traps built by non-human hunters.

262
00:19:25,400 --> 00:19:31,400
More than 35,000 species of spiders have been identified, with new ones being discovered constantly.

263
00:19:31,401 --> 00:19:37,400
They're all predators, and most use their spinnerets to make silken and often gooey traps.

264
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:44,400
But while the typical orb web, strung up to catch insects that accidentally fly into it, is an impressive engineering feat,

265
00:19:45,400 --> 00:19:47,400
it's really a passive form of hunting.

266
00:19:47,401 --> 00:19:53,280
The ogre-faced spider takes a much more proactive approach to snagging a meal.

267
00:19:53,920 --> 00:19:58,180
The ogre-faced spider is so named because of its remarkable eyes.

268
00:19:58,300 --> 00:20:01,940
Most spiders have eight eyes, and in the case of the ogre-faced spider,

269
00:20:02,100 --> 00:20:03,800
two of these eyes are huge.

270
00:20:04,380 --> 00:20:05,700
They're much bigger than the others.

271
00:20:05,980 --> 00:20:08,860
They can see a clear image under very low light.

272
00:20:09,180 --> 00:20:12,620
This is a preserved specimen of an ogre-faced spider.

273
00:20:13,220 --> 00:20:17,460
The scientific name Dinopus means horrible eyes, and it's very aptly named.

274
00:20:19,280 --> 00:20:22,300
Ogre-faced spiders live throughout the warmer parts of the world,

275
00:20:22,301 --> 00:20:25,440
from the southern United States to southern Africa and Australia.

276
00:20:26,120 --> 00:20:30,560
Like most orb-web-building spiders, they use their spinnerets to weave a small web,

277
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:32,020
but with a big difference.

278
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:35,340
The ogre-faced spider is unique in the world of spiders

279
00:20:35,341 --> 00:20:38,980
in that it constructs a web that it physically holds in its legs.

280
00:20:38,981 --> 00:20:44,780
The web that it constructs is a squarish structure with zigzag lines going in varying directions.

281
00:20:45,260 --> 00:20:49,240
The spider then hangs in an area where insects are walking by.

282
00:20:49,500 --> 00:20:55,120
When an hapless insect happens to walk by, what the ogre-faced spider does is it takes its square silk frame,

283
00:20:55,580 --> 00:20:57,360
flings it physically on the insect,

284
00:20:57,660 --> 00:21:03,320
and then it raises it off the ground and starts jiggling it back and forth.

285
00:21:03,321 --> 00:21:07,360
The more it jiggles the insect, the more entangled it becomes in the silk framework

286
00:21:07,361 --> 00:21:10,960
that allows it then to be bitten and eventually the animals consume.

287
00:21:12,300 --> 00:21:17,100
The ogre-faced spider isn't alone in its ability to use its web in an innovative way.

288
00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:22,980
Another amazing arachnid uses a single line of silk with the skill of a rodeo champion.

289
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:31,040
The bolus spider is so named because of an analogy to Argentinian gauchos

290
00:21:31,041 --> 00:21:36,240
who use a bolus, which is a leather thong with a stone at each end.

291
00:21:36,241 --> 00:21:39,680
They swing this around and they hurl it at running animals.

292
00:21:39,880 --> 00:21:44,860
It entangles their legs, brings them down, and lets the cowboy, the gaucho, catch them.

293
00:21:45,480 --> 00:21:48,280
The bolus spider does something a little bit like that.

294
00:21:48,520 --> 00:21:52,580
The bolus spider makes a remarkable catching web.

295
00:21:53,060 --> 00:22:00,240
It's a single silken line at the tip of which is a large blob of sticky silk, gluey silk.

296
00:22:00,241 --> 00:22:04,240
The spider itself produces a smell of perfume,

297
00:22:04,241 --> 00:22:08,240
which is like the sex perfume of moths.

298
00:22:08,241 --> 00:22:11,240
And so male moths will fly upwind

299
00:22:11,241 --> 00:22:13,240
toward this wonderful perfume,

300
00:22:13,241 --> 00:22:17,240
hoping to find a female moth with which they can mate.

301
00:22:17,241 --> 00:22:19,240
And what the bolo spider does is,

302
00:22:19,241 --> 00:22:21,240
in order to both get the perfume spread around

303
00:22:21,241 --> 00:22:24,240
and in order to catch their prey, is they spin it.

304
00:22:24,241 --> 00:22:26,240
They spin the line with this gob of glue

305
00:22:26,241 --> 00:22:28,240
and pheromones on the bottom of it.

306
00:22:28,241 --> 00:22:30,240
When the moth comes over, whack, it gets hit.

307
00:22:30,241 --> 00:22:33,240
They drag them in and suck them dry.

308
00:22:35,240 --> 00:22:37,240
Not all spiders use their web-spinning capabilities

309
00:22:37,241 --> 00:22:41,240
to create sticky death traps to snare their prey.

310
00:22:41,241 --> 00:22:44,240
Trapdoor spiders are famous for their secret hatches,

311
00:22:44,241 --> 00:22:47,240
from which they make surprise attacks.

312
00:22:47,241 --> 00:22:51,240
If that was too fast for you, here's an instant replay.

313
00:22:51,241 --> 00:23:00,460
This is a ball of adobe soil. It's very hard.

314
00:23:00,920 --> 00:23:04,600
And in this ball of soil is the burrow of a trapdoor spider.

315
00:23:04,980 --> 00:23:07,100
You can see the door falling open like this.

316
00:23:07,840 --> 00:23:10,660
Trapdoor spiders burrow into the ground,

317
00:23:10,840 --> 00:23:14,880
or they occupy crevices and rock walls or tree trunks.

318
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:18,220
And they line their burrow with silk,

319
00:23:18,221 --> 00:23:22,620
and at the end of this burrow, they produce this remarkable trapdoor,

320
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:27,660
which, when closed, makes the burrow very, very difficult to see.

321
00:23:28,860 --> 00:23:33,540
It often takes a trained eye to spot the door designed to blend in with the surroundings,

322
00:23:34,020 --> 00:23:37,920
and many trapdoor spiders are less than hospitable to unwelcome visitors.

323
00:23:38,900 --> 00:23:42,740
Quite often in the field, when you come up and try to open the burrow door,

324
00:23:43,420 --> 00:23:46,540
you'll sometimes discover that the resident spider is on the other side

325
00:23:46,541 --> 00:23:48,840
having a tug of war with you to keep the door shut.

326
00:23:49,460 --> 00:23:52,760
And that's not just because they're concerned about you, a human.

327
00:23:53,120 --> 00:23:55,300
The reality is that these spiders have enemies.

328
00:23:55,540 --> 00:23:59,600
There are wasps that physically land on the ground near the burrow doorway.

329
00:24:00,120 --> 00:24:02,980
They physically then try to lift the burrow and go inside,

330
00:24:03,460 --> 00:24:05,500
sting the resident spider and paralyze it.

331
00:24:05,501 --> 00:24:10,220
And we'll try this with this particular one and see what in fact it does.

332
00:24:10,221 --> 00:24:18,760
and indeed it is struggling to keep the door shut against my probe.

333
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:25,040
Back in the 1940s, one of these burrows of a large trapdoor spider such as this

334
00:24:25,041 --> 00:24:27,100
was wired up to a grocery scale

335
00:24:27,101 --> 00:24:33,360
and it was discovered that the spider would not release its hold

336
00:24:33,361 --> 00:24:36,900
until a force equivalent to 140 times its body weight

337
00:24:36,901 --> 00:24:39,060
had been applied to the burrow entryway.

338
00:24:40,220 --> 00:24:45,400
Trapdoor spiders are also powerful diggers and excavate burrows up to a foot or more deep.

339
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:50,340
Yeah, trapdoor spiders have a rake on their jaws.

340
00:24:50,560 --> 00:24:52,780
This rake is used to dig in the ground.

341
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:58,440
They will scrape particles of earth from the walls of the burrow,

342
00:24:58,600 --> 00:25:00,960
and they'll carry it to the end of the burrow,

343
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:04,920
where they can actually sometimes throw it for a considerable distance

344
00:25:04,921 --> 00:25:11,020
so that no buildup of debris around the burrow would serve to give it away to any potential enemy.

345
00:25:11,420 --> 00:25:15,900
As the burrow is being excavated, the spider uses its spinnerets to line it with silk.

346
00:25:16,400 --> 00:25:18,760
This not only helps hold the earthen walls in place,

347
00:25:19,200 --> 00:25:21,820
but it also provides a comfy nest to raise their young

348
00:25:21,821 --> 00:25:24,900
and enables the spider to quickly travel up to its door,

349
00:25:25,300 --> 00:25:28,760
which is made of a mixture of silk, earth, and other organic material.

350
00:25:29,140 --> 00:25:34,580
There's particular attention paid to the edge to make sure that it fits snugly with the rim of the burrow.

351
00:25:34,920 --> 00:25:40,380
If these are constructed perfectly and the door is shut, water will actually tend not to go inside.

352
00:25:40,760 --> 00:25:46,680
Most trapdoor spiders hunt at night and wait patiently behind their door, sometimes with it slightly ajar.

353
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:53,960
Certain types of trapdoor spiders lay silken lines that radiate out from their door to act as tripwires.

354
00:25:55,280 --> 00:26:03,360
If something walks by and taps or stumbles on one of these lines of web, the vibrations let the spider know dinner has arrived.

355
00:26:03,361 --> 00:26:11,140
Others have no such lines, but all have extremely acute senses of vibration detection.

356
00:26:11,980 --> 00:26:18,080
They can locate, range, and assess the size of any passing creature.

357
00:26:18,340 --> 00:26:22,540
They can tell from the vibrations whether this is an ant or a beetle,

358
00:26:22,700 --> 00:26:26,260
or they can determine if it's something larger, like a frog or a lizard.

359
00:26:26,940 --> 00:26:29,900
And if they determine that, they'll keep the door shut tight.

360
00:26:29,901 --> 00:26:35,240
Trapdoor spiders have carved out a comfortable and secretive niche for themselves behind

361
00:26:35,241 --> 00:26:37,940
their cleverly devised and disguised lairs.

362
00:26:37,941 --> 00:26:43,580
But they aren't the only hunters lying in wait beneath the ground.

363
00:26:43,581 --> 00:26:49,400
While in its larval stage, one variety of a dragonfly-like insect, called an antlion,

364
00:26:49,401 --> 00:26:54,860
digs a cone-shaped deathtrap in fine soil or sand.

365
00:26:54,861 --> 00:26:59,860
When digging its pit, the antlion uses its head and jaws to flick dirt out,

366
00:26:59,861 --> 00:27:04,620
but does it in such a way that the walls of its crater are lined with finer soil particles,

367
00:27:04,621 --> 00:27:07,620
resulting in a steeper and more slippery slope.

368
00:27:07,621 --> 00:27:12,340
It throws them at a 45-degree angle, which is the way in which you can get the furthest

369
00:27:12,341 --> 00:27:16,180
distance for the amount of effort you put into your throwing.

370
00:27:16,181 --> 00:27:19,200
Then the larger particles travel further.

371
00:27:19,201 --> 00:27:24,020
So just by throwing the particles out, it throws the bigger particles further than it

372
00:27:24,021 --> 00:27:28,020
it throws the smaller particles.

373
00:27:28,021 --> 00:27:30,760
Once the antlion finishes digging its pit,

374
00:27:30,761 --> 00:27:33,780
it buries itself at the bottom with only its head and jaws

375
00:27:33,781 --> 00:27:35,180
exposed.

376
00:27:35,181 --> 00:27:40,160
And eventually, an ant or a caterpillar or something

377
00:27:40,161 --> 00:27:44,280
will walk through that area and stumble

378
00:27:44,281 --> 00:27:46,360
over the edge of one of the pits.

379
00:27:46,361 --> 00:27:48,640
And as it starts tumbling down the sides,

380
00:27:48,641 --> 00:27:52,300
the larvae will start flicking sand up at it

381
00:27:52,301 --> 00:27:55,260
to make sure that it goes all the way to the bottom,

382
00:27:55,261 --> 00:27:57,440
and then it will drag it under the surface.

383
00:27:58,860 --> 00:28:01,200
The aggressive antlion then seizes its prey

384
00:28:01,201 --> 00:28:03,860
with its enormous, piercing, sucking mandibles,

385
00:28:04,720 --> 00:28:08,320
paralyzes it with poison injected at the first bite,

386
00:28:08,321 --> 00:28:10,700
and proceeds to suck out its vital juices.

387
00:28:11,640 --> 00:28:14,400
When finished, it throws the drained, lifeless body

388
00:28:14,401 --> 00:28:17,340
out of its pit and lays in wait for its next victim.

389
00:28:19,060 --> 00:28:21,000
Fortunately for all of us bigger members

390
00:28:21,001 --> 00:28:24,040
of the animal kingdom, these monstrous trappers only grow

391
00:28:24,041 --> 00:28:26,560
to a maximum size of about half an inch.

392
00:28:28,620 --> 00:28:31,980
It's a dangerous world out there, but for every creature

393
00:28:31,981 --> 00:28:34,880
that has figured out how to build a better mousetrap,

394
00:28:34,881 --> 00:28:37,000
there is another that has designed a home

395
00:28:37,001 --> 00:28:38,980
to keep the predators away.

396
00:28:40,340 --> 00:28:43,720
Most spiders have a lifespan of one or two years.

397
00:28:43,721 --> 00:28:46,220
Thanks in part to their slower metabolisms,

398
00:28:46,221 --> 00:28:49,000
trapdoor spiders can live up to 20 years.

399
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:56,000
Most people are content to live in homes made from a variety of building materials.

400
00:28:56,001 --> 00:29:04,000
But for members of the bird world, that variety, the twigs, leaves and grasses, is a matter of convenience.

401
00:29:04,001 --> 00:29:11,000
But just as some people choose to be non-conformists or have to improvise because of climate or availability of supplies,

402
00:29:11,001 --> 00:29:15,000
many animal engineers build distinctive dwellings as well.

403
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:23,340
While most birds build a nest up in a tree,

404
00:29:24,660 --> 00:29:28,420
woodpeckers use their strong pointed beaks like chisels and crowbars

405
00:29:28,421 --> 00:29:30,900
to carve out a home in the tree itself.

406
00:29:32,180 --> 00:29:36,300
Males and females would work together to excavate a hole suitable for nesting.

407
00:29:37,360 --> 00:29:39,840
These fairly small birds weigh only a few ounces,

408
00:29:40,500 --> 00:29:44,740
but are able to jackhammer their way even into hardwood trees like oak and maple.

409
00:29:45,280 --> 00:29:49,500
As the bird hits its head, essentially, against the tree trunk

410
00:29:49,501 --> 00:29:55,460
by trying to dig a hole by hitting very hard the point of the chisel against the tree,

411
00:29:56,180 --> 00:30:02,760
its brain would get shaken round if it wasn't for some special design features in the skull.

412
00:30:03,480 --> 00:30:07,920
The way in which the beak is inset into the skull

413
00:30:07,921 --> 00:30:13,260
ensures that the shock waves largely bypass the brain

414
00:30:13,261 --> 00:30:18,500
and also there are specialist muscles which act as shock absorbers.

415
00:30:19,500 --> 00:30:25,340
In Arizona's treeless desert, Gila woodpeckers have found a substitute place to excavate a home.

416
00:30:26,380 --> 00:30:32,760
If you look behind me, you see the saguaro cactus, the icon of the Sonoran Desert here in southern Arizona.

417
00:30:32,761 --> 00:30:37,220
This plant is a very common home of the Gila woodpecker.

418
00:30:37,620 --> 00:30:41,580
It's a two-inch, roughly wide hole dug into the plant,

419
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:46,000
and the hole looks like it goes straight in from perhaps this viewpoint,

420
00:30:46,001 --> 00:30:52,140
but drops down into a boot-shaped or perhaps a bottleneck gorge-shaped cavity.

421
00:30:52,141 --> 00:30:57,940
A great advantage of nesting in the saguaro cactus is that it provides a cooling effect.

422
00:30:58,120 --> 00:31:05,460
The tissues are moisture-filled, perhaps 80% water content.

423
00:31:05,680 --> 00:31:13,280
That provides a good barrier against an outdoor ambient temperature that might be up to 110 degrees.

424
00:31:13,281 --> 00:31:20,280
Many species of much larger birds called hornbills also nest in holes in trees,

425
00:31:20,281 --> 00:31:26,280
but they're unable to chip out their own homes and must find already existing cavities.

426
00:31:26,281 --> 00:31:31,280
In the predator-filled tropics, hornbills go one step further than woodpeckers.

427
00:31:31,281 --> 00:31:33,280
They build a front door.

428
00:31:33,281 --> 00:31:40,280
The way in which they do this is that males go out and swallow mud

429
00:31:40,281 --> 00:31:43,500
and then regurgitate to the female this sticky mud,

430
00:31:44,080 --> 00:31:50,100
and the female then builds it up into a wall,

431
00:31:50,200 --> 00:31:52,980
which is almost complete except for a narrow vertical slit.

432
00:31:53,680 --> 00:31:55,380
Locked away from the outside world,

433
00:31:55,800 --> 00:31:58,880
the female relies on the male to feed her through the narrow opening.

434
00:32:01,460 --> 00:32:04,480
Once the eggs hatch, the female breaks out

435
00:32:04,481 --> 00:32:06,880
and works with the male to feed their growing brood.

436
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:10,080
While woodpeckers and hornbills go to a lot of trouble

437
00:32:10,081 --> 00:32:12,020
to construct their nests within trees.

438
00:32:12,760 --> 00:32:14,460
Another winged animal has found a way

439
00:32:14,461 --> 00:32:16,240
to quickly create makeshift shelters

440
00:32:16,241 --> 00:32:17,480
out of just the leaves.

441
00:32:18,920 --> 00:32:22,160
The tent is a longtime standard in survivalist gear.

442
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:25,140
But tent bats discovered the value

443
00:32:25,141 --> 00:32:26,960
of this simple yet effective structure

444
00:32:26,961 --> 00:32:28,140
long before we did.

445
00:32:29,140 --> 00:32:30,820
They are probably, in my opinion,

446
00:32:31,320 --> 00:32:33,280
amongst the most beautiful of all bats.

447
00:32:33,281 --> 00:32:35,600
Some species are pure white

448
00:32:35,601 --> 00:32:39,020
with beautiful yellow faces and yellow ears.

449
00:32:39,021 --> 00:32:48,210
And they'll not only take a group of leaves and cut the veins and then pull them down so that the leaf makes an automatic little teepee kind of structure or a tent,

450
00:32:48,211 --> 00:32:51,460
but they'll also do it with big plants like banana plants.

451
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:58,740
In order to make this kind of leaf into a shelter, they bite through the veins that branch off the midrib that runs down the center of it.

452
00:32:58,741 --> 00:33:05,660
Starting probably up this end, they would go and bite through that vein and that vein and that vein,

453
00:33:05,840 --> 00:33:11,440
fairly close to the midrib there, until doing it all the way down here, that side would collapse.

454
00:33:12,480 --> 00:33:15,980
And then similarly on the other side, so the other side would collapse,

455
00:33:16,420 --> 00:33:21,040
to create a kind of folded structure like that, giving it protection against the rain.

456
00:33:21,540 --> 00:33:24,080
The bats then roost up in the apex of the tent.

457
00:33:25,020 --> 00:33:27,780
It offers protection from sunshine and rain.

458
00:33:28,740 --> 00:33:34,320
The lack of a floor enables the bats to keep an eye out for any predators that may be approaching below.

459
00:33:37,320 --> 00:33:42,480
For those of us with a sense of wanderlust, we have invented trailers and mobile homes.

460
00:33:43,040 --> 00:33:46,740
For travel in hostile environments, we have created armored personnel carriers.

461
00:33:48,140 --> 00:33:53,180
In streams and ponds, the aquatic caddisworm, the larvae of the caddisfly,

462
00:33:53,700 --> 00:33:56,600
has to make its own protective mobile home to survive.

463
00:33:56,601 --> 00:34:01,600
Hatched in the water, the defenceless worm is a favourite food of fish.

464
00:34:02,600 --> 00:34:07,400
Using its sticky saliva, it glues together a covering of rocks and other available materials.

465
00:34:08,100 --> 00:34:15,600
This caddis case I have here almost certainly illustrates a design which has anti-predator features.

466
00:34:16,520 --> 00:34:21,600
So what you see is the tube in which the animal lives is this middle dark bit here,

467
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:23,660
and you can just see the entrance of the tube there.

468
00:34:23,661 --> 00:34:29,400
But stuck onto the front end, or projecting well beyond the front end of the tube,

469
00:34:29,580 --> 00:34:32,540
is quite a large, thick stick.

470
00:34:33,240 --> 00:34:38,400
And then projecting beyond the back end are three thin sticks,

471
00:34:38,800 --> 00:34:42,400
so that you can see that the overall length of the structure

472
00:34:42,401 --> 00:34:46,780
is almost double the length of the cadicit larva itself.

473
00:34:47,060 --> 00:34:51,460
Now, if you imagine a fish with a relatively small mouth

474
00:34:51,461 --> 00:34:54,040
trying to actually grab hold of that and swallow it,

475
00:34:54,760 --> 00:35:00,360
then it's much more difficult with these bits stuck on projecting from either end.

476
00:35:01,280 --> 00:35:04,820
Once the caddis worm metamorphoses into a caddis fly,

477
00:35:05,740 --> 00:35:08,620
its mobile home is cast off into nature's junkyard.

478
00:35:09,200 --> 00:35:12,540
But as impressive as this bit of insect engineering is,

479
00:35:13,040 --> 00:35:16,160
it's dwarfed by the efforts of ants.

480
00:35:17,480 --> 00:35:20,800
It takes hornbill couples an average of three to seven days

481
00:35:20,801 --> 00:35:22,800
to seal up the front of their nest cavities.

482
00:35:23,560 --> 00:35:27,720
Depending on the species, females can remain inside for three to four months.

483
00:35:31,100 --> 00:35:34,720
Our ability to work as a unified, multi-skilled force

484
00:35:34,721 --> 00:35:36,720
has resulted in engineering miracles.

485
00:35:38,120 --> 00:35:41,860
Strangely, the closest parallel to these sorts of complex creations

486
00:35:41,861 --> 00:35:46,180
exists in some of the least human and tiniest inhabitants of our planet,

487
00:35:46,181 --> 00:35:47,460
the ants.

488
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:54,800
There are over 8,000 species of ants.

489
00:35:54,801 --> 00:35:59,800
Some challenge our preconceptions about these very industrious insects.

490
00:35:59,801 --> 00:36:02,800
Weaver ants live in the rainforest,

491
00:36:02,801 --> 00:36:05,800
where it is impractical to build a typical ant colony

492
00:36:05,801 --> 00:36:08,800
beneath the often rain-soaked ground.

493
00:36:08,801 --> 00:36:13,800
Instead, they climb into the trees to craft their houses out of leaves.

494
00:36:13,801 --> 00:36:17,800
What they've learned is that they can go up and, as a team,

495
00:36:17,801 --> 00:36:21,300
pull leaves together, then they grab their larvae

496
00:36:21,301 --> 00:36:23,600
and squeeze them like a toothpaste tube,

497
00:36:23,601 --> 00:36:26,600
and that actually creates this silken web

498
00:36:26,601 --> 00:36:29,640
that they actually use to sew the leaves together.

499
00:36:29,641 --> 00:36:31,640
And so what happens, they form a structure,

500
00:36:31,641 --> 00:36:34,040
a very solid structure, high up in the tree,

501
00:36:34,041 --> 00:36:35,560
and it's quite impressive.

502
00:36:35,561 --> 00:36:38,460
Squeezing the silk from the larvae

503
00:36:38,461 --> 00:36:41,260
without harming them to sew their nests together

504
00:36:41,261 --> 00:36:43,800
is a remarkably complex behavior.

505
00:36:43,801 --> 00:36:48,800
Weaver ants also exhibit interesting defensive maneuvers.

506
00:36:48,801 --> 00:36:52,800
If their nest is disturbed, the ants make an intimidating sound

507
00:36:52,801 --> 00:36:54,800
by tapping on the leaves.

508
00:36:54,801 --> 00:36:58,800
When a person opens up these weaver nests,

509
00:36:58,801 --> 00:37:03,800
they'll find that the adults are nervously tapping.

510
00:37:03,801 --> 00:37:04,800
I've seen it.

511
00:37:04,801 --> 00:37:09,800
They'll make quite a ruckus as they nervously tap the substrate

512
00:37:09,801 --> 00:37:11,800
of the leaves.

513
00:37:11,801 --> 00:37:13,580
It could be for communication.

514
00:37:13,760 --> 00:37:15,680
It could be to release a chemical.

515
00:37:16,460 --> 00:37:20,160
It could be that the noise is just meant to drive off a predator.

516
00:37:20,760 --> 00:37:25,040
The whole nest starts to click as they're shaking on the leaves.

517
00:37:25,041 --> 00:37:30,720
And then they all run out and look at you with their legs, front legs kind of standing out this way saying,

518
00:37:30,960 --> 00:37:32,140
come a little closer, my friend.

519
00:37:32,460 --> 00:37:38,280
And then if they bite you, they actually spray an acid that makes it feel like you're dissolving.

520
00:37:38,281 --> 00:37:43,280
And it's one of the things that keeps people and other things away from them.

521
00:37:43,281 --> 00:37:48,040
Army ants are also experts at construction.

522
00:37:48,041 --> 00:37:51,440
Only their creations are built out of their own bodies.

523
00:37:51,441 --> 00:37:56,320
Like a troop of precision acrobats, these ants quickly communicate and coordinate to

524
00:37:56,321 --> 00:38:03,640
assemble themselves into ladders, roads, and other structures by linking their legs together.

525
00:38:03,641 --> 00:38:07,940
And on the march, if they come to a gap that needs to be crossed, they will build a living

526
00:38:07,941 --> 00:38:08,940
bridge.

527
00:38:08,941 --> 00:38:12,980
So what will happen is they'll start to form like building blocks.

528
00:38:12,981 --> 00:38:17,320
One will start, second, and they start to build up, and then it starts to form out.

529
00:38:17,321 --> 00:38:21,400
And then you start getting a curtain, you'll get one or two ants that will be stretched

530
00:38:21,401 --> 00:38:23,920
out as thin as possible, just holding on.

531
00:38:23,921 --> 00:38:26,240
The next one will come up and then they'll add another third block.

532
00:38:26,241 --> 00:38:30,520
And before long, you've got this actual bridge, and then the rest of the collars comes racing

533
00:38:30,521 --> 00:38:34,114
across, and then once they're through, they dismantle the same way.

534
00:38:34,115 --> 00:38:35,240
The back one lets go,

535
00:38:35,360 --> 00:38:38,880
then they carry it back on until they cross the obstacle.

536
00:38:40,200 --> 00:38:44,680
One of the most important structures they assemble themselves into is a home for their

537
00:38:44,681 --> 00:38:45,381
queen.

538
00:38:45,382 --> 00:38:50,600
Army ants are nomadic, and their queen must be protected and given a place to replenish

539
00:38:50,601 --> 00:38:51,301
the workforce.

540
00:38:51,840 --> 00:38:53,280
They're called bivouacs.

541
00:38:53,281 --> 00:38:56,880
And the way they do this is by one ant hooking on to another

542
00:38:56,881 --> 00:39:02,920
ant and another ant hooking on to another ant and making curtains of ants that form

543
00:39:02,921 --> 00:39:07,980
this bull that comes down out of a tree or in a hollow in an old branch.

544
00:39:07,981 --> 00:39:10,560
And then they make platforms inside of that.

545
00:39:10,561 --> 00:39:14,880
And on that, they keep their babies and the queen and their pupa, et cetera.

546
00:39:14,881 --> 00:39:16,640
So this is actually their home.

547
00:39:16,641 --> 00:39:21,200
They'll form a bivouac in a hurry if they sense their queen is in danger.

548
00:39:21,201 --> 00:39:23,640
And the curtains of ants are like living chain mail.

549
00:39:23,641 --> 00:39:27,640
It's very tough. It's like Velcro when you pull them apart.

550
00:39:27,641 --> 00:39:32,640
And yet they form these bridges and curtains with their bodies and their legs.

551
00:39:32,641 --> 00:39:36,640
It is engineering and it's in a grand scale.

552
00:39:36,641 --> 00:39:40,640
If army ants are the insect world's most amazing acrobats,

553
00:39:40,641 --> 00:39:45,640
leafcutter ants are horticultural geniuses when it comes to farming.

554
00:39:45,641 --> 00:39:50,640
Their unusual multi-stepped method of harvesting one crop in order to grow another,

555
00:39:50,641 --> 00:39:53,640
a certain fungus for which they've developed an appetite,

556
00:39:53,641 --> 00:39:57,640
rivals human agricultural accomplishments.

557
00:39:57,641 --> 00:39:59,640
Leafcutter ants are incredible.

558
00:39:59,641 --> 00:40:03,640
They have achieved a very high sophistication in terms of farming.

559
00:40:03,641 --> 00:40:06,640
What they've done is they've learned to take food,

560
00:40:06,641 --> 00:40:10,640
a different variety of greens, and take them back to the nest,

561
00:40:10,641 --> 00:40:15,640
mash them up, and in conjunction with this fungus that they live with,

562
00:40:15,641 --> 00:40:17,640
it's a symbiotic relationship.

563
00:40:17,641 --> 00:40:21,320
The fungus can't live without them, nor they without the fungus.

564
00:40:22,720 --> 00:40:27,340
It takes a lot of freshly harvested greens to grow enough fungus to feed the colony.

565
00:40:28,080 --> 00:40:30,760
So scouts are constantly searching for new supplies.

566
00:40:32,100 --> 00:40:35,280
When a leafcutter scout finds a suitable bush or tree,

567
00:40:35,880 --> 00:40:39,320
it lays a scent trail to let the forager ants know where it is.

568
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:43,640
The ants that cut the vegetation have two sets of jaws.

569
00:40:44,300 --> 00:40:47,500
The outer pair is used for carrying objects and for digging.

570
00:40:47,640 --> 00:40:49,980
And the inner pair is used for chewing.

571
00:40:50,580 --> 00:40:54,580
So as they cut, they wiggle, you know, they move their jaws back and forth to cut.

572
00:40:55,040 --> 00:41:00,540
But what makes it even more efficient is that they vibrate like a kitchen knife,

573
00:41:00,680 --> 00:41:03,060
and that vibration makes these work quicker.

574
00:41:03,140 --> 00:41:05,360
So bzzz, bzzz, bzzz.

575
00:41:05,440 --> 00:41:08,900
And that's how they can cut the leaves much more efficiently

576
00:41:08,901 --> 00:41:11,220
than they would if they were just biting them with their jaws.

577
00:41:11,920 --> 00:41:14,980
Once the leaves are cut, the ants pick up the pieces

578
00:41:14,981 --> 00:41:18,320
and begin the often very long trek back to the nest.

579
00:41:18,880 --> 00:41:21,020
The leafcutter ant, when it's moving,

580
00:41:21,400 --> 00:41:24,740
actually will form a path, like a highway in the forest.

581
00:41:25,100 --> 00:41:29,400
It'll denude this whole area of grass, of leaves, of twigs,

582
00:41:29,740 --> 00:41:30,620
and it'll just be dirt.

583
00:41:30,840 --> 00:41:31,640
It's a big highway.

584
00:41:31,760 --> 00:41:33,300
Some of these are a couple inches across.

585
00:41:34,780 --> 00:41:36,720
When the foragers get to the nest,

586
00:41:36,721 --> 00:41:39,060
they hand off the leaves to smaller ants

587
00:41:39,061 --> 00:41:41,760
that then rush them to one of the many culture gardens.

588
00:41:41,761 --> 00:41:48,060
The leaves are chewed into smaller and smaller fragments by smaller and smaller ants, then

589
00:41:48,061 --> 00:41:50,940
placed into the nutrient demanding fungus.

590
00:41:50,941 --> 00:41:57,460
This is a Arizona, it's called Atta Mexicana, but it's actually an Arizona leafcutter ant,

591
00:41:57,461 --> 00:42:00,520
and it's one of the biggest ones found in the United States.

592
00:42:00,521 --> 00:42:03,980
And if you look right there, you can see the queen.

593
00:42:03,981 --> 00:42:05,120
She's the founder.

594
00:42:05,121 --> 00:42:08,820
When I picked her up out in the desert, she just had a little ball of fungus under her

595
00:42:08,821 --> 00:42:14,240
jaw and I put her in a container and she raised the fungus up and now it's turned into this

596
00:42:14,241 --> 00:42:19,460
ball of fungus. And this colony here is about three months old.

597
00:42:19,461 --> 00:42:24,440
Like most social insects, one leafcutter queen can generate an enormous number of new individuals

598
00:42:24,441 --> 00:42:25,440
over time.

599
00:42:25,441 --> 00:42:31,220
Probably the biggest social insect colonies you get are found among ants and leafcutter

600
00:42:31,221 --> 00:42:33,359
ants are probably supreme among those.

601
00:42:33,360 --> 00:42:36,060
So you're getting colony sizes of something like

602
00:42:36,061 --> 00:42:37,269
five to eight million.

603
00:42:37,270 --> 00:42:41,060
Now, I come from Scotland and the population of Scotland is only five

604
00:42:41,061 --> 00:42:42,553
and a half million.

605
00:42:42,554 --> 00:42:47,820
So we're talking about in one building as many insects as there are

606
00:42:47,821 --> 00:42:52,100
people in the whole of Scotland.

607
00:42:52,101 --> 00:42:57,340
To accommodate a population of this size, a long-established leafcutter colony's underground

608
00:42:57,341 --> 00:42:59,640
nest can become gigantic.

609
00:42:59,641 --> 00:43:02,408
Think about yourself as having x-ray eyes.

610
00:43:02,409 --> 00:43:05,440
What you would see are fungus balls that range

611
00:43:05,441 --> 00:43:10,180
from the size of about an orange to about the size of a basketball, and there would

612
00:43:10,181 --> 00:43:12,980
be thousands of them all laid out on the ground.

613
00:43:12,981 --> 00:43:17,280
And then there would be these tunnels that go down even deeper into these midden piles

614
00:43:17,281 --> 00:43:21,620
where the old fungus is thrown away that's no longer good for food.

615
00:43:21,621 --> 00:43:26,320
And some of those chambers that are deep underground are so big that you can actually stand them

616
00:43:26,321 --> 00:43:27,320
on.

617
00:43:27,321 --> 00:43:30,280
And that's how far, and they go down and down, and nobody's ever really dug one of these

618
00:43:30,281 --> 00:43:34,200
giant colonies all the way to the bottom because the hole gets so deep that they're afraid it's

619
00:43:34,201 --> 00:43:36,740
going to cave in on.

620
00:43:36,741 --> 00:43:38,980
Their colonies can flourish because leaf cutters

621
00:43:38,981 --> 00:43:41,820
vigilantly protect their fungus cultures from disease

622
00:43:41,821 --> 00:43:43,320
and invading fungi pests.

623
00:43:45,740 --> 00:43:47,380
In addition to physically removing

624
00:43:47,381 --> 00:43:49,920
any unwanted invading varieties of fungi

625
00:43:49,921 --> 00:43:52,580
that may start growing in their garden,

626
00:43:52,581 --> 00:43:56,080
these ants employ antibiotics produced by bacteria

627
00:43:56,081 --> 00:44:01,160
living on their bodies to help keep their crop healthy.

628
00:44:01,161 --> 00:44:05,080
This balancing act between ant, bacteria, and fungus,

629
00:44:05,081 --> 00:44:07,620
along with their backbreaking fieldwork,

630
00:44:07,621 --> 00:44:11,880
makes leafcutters one of nature's most impressive engineers.

